@startuml
title Default Remote-First Conflict Handling
start

:Begin normal remote-first reconciliation;
note right
  Operating mode:
  onedrive ~--sync
  or a normal ~--monitor reconciliation cycle
  without ~--local-first.

  Online changes are examined first, but remote-first
  is an ordering rule - not permission to destroy
  unique local data.
end note

:Enumerate Microsoft OneDrive online state;
:Classify and reconcile incoming online item;
if (Incoming item replaces file content?) then (yes)
  :Download candidate into private .partial staging file;
  :Validate final HTTP result, size/hash policy and metadata;
  note right
    The online replacement is staged privately first.
    The existing canonical local file is still present.
  end note

  if (Canonical local file exists?) then (yes)
    :Read canonical local content at commit boundary;
    :Read last applied DB baseline when available;
    note right
      Three states are compared here:
      1. current local canonical content
      2. last successfully applied DB baseline
      3. incoming authoritative online content
    end note
    if (Canonical matches incoming online content?) then (yes)
      :No preservation required;
      note right
        Local bytes already equal the incoming online version.
        Replacing them cannot lose unique local content.
      end note
    else (no)
      if (Canonical matches last applied DB baseline?) then (yes)
        :No independent local content change;\nno preservation required;
        note right
          The local file still represents the last applied state.
          It has not independently changed since the previous sync.
        end note
      else (no)
        :Create and verify non-destructive safeBackup copy;
        note right
          LOCAL DATA LOSS PREVENTION

          The local file contains content that is different from both
          the last applied DB baseline and the incoming online version.
          Preserve those unique local bytes before replacement.
        end note
        if (Preservation fails?) then (yes)
          :Reject replacement and retain canonical / DB state;
          note right
            A required safeBackup is a commit prerequisite.
            Failure to preserve means the online replacement
            must NOT overwrite the canonical local file.
          end note
          stop
        endif
      endif
    endif
    :Recheck canonical did not change during commit preparation;
  endif

  :Atomically promote validated .partial to canonical path;
  :Apply authoritative online timestamp and metadata;
  :Save applied online state in DB;
  note right
    Result:
    - online version becomes the canonical local file
    - any unique previous local version survives as safeBackup
    - DB now describes the content actually committed locally
  end note
else (no)
  :Apply directory, metadata, move or delete handling as required;
endif

:Run known-state database consistency pass;
:Scan for new local data when uploads are enabled;
note right
  A newly created safeBackup is a local file and may be uploaded
  as a new item during this scan unless excluded with skip_file.
end note
:Perform final online true-up where applicable;
stop
@enduml
